Vitamin B12 / Iron Intramuscular Injection
General Practice Other
Established B12/iron deficiency, Indication for parenteral administration, Intramuscular injection, Post-injection observation

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-050
Reason for Visit: Urgent request for administration of a scheduled, critical intramuscular vitamin injection due to profound, incapacitating fatigue and an inability to attend a daytime clinic.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient with severe, established pernicious anemia who had missed their scheduled B12 injection by several weeks due to severe agoraphobia. The patient is now experiencing acute, profound lethargy, dizziness, and parasthesias, rendering them completely unable to leave the house. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to assess for severe anemic decompensation, perform the immediate deep intramuscular injection to reverse the acute neurological and hematological symptoms, and safely observe the patient. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in worsening neurological deficits and a high probability of collapse or emergency department presentation.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I'm so exhausted I can barely lift my arms, my feet are tingling constantly, and I haven't had my B12 shot in six weeks because I couldn't leave the house."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an established confirmed diagnosis of specific vitamin B12 deficiency anaemia (pernicious anemia). There is a documented clinical indication for direct parenteral administration rather than oral supplementation due to intrinsic factor deficiency. The patient reports a sensation of generalized severe fatigue, lethargy, and worsening peripheral neuropathy (tingling in feet) prior to the scheduled administration. The presentation requires the preparation of the specific medication for deep intramuscular injection. The patient understands the expected localized mild discomfort or stinging at the specific injection site.
Functional Status: Severely restricted by profound fatigue and psychological barriers (agoraphobia); largely bedbound today.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of profound medical neglect due to severe, unmanaged psychological barriers to accessing routine daytime care.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Pale, appearing exhausted, speaking slowly, resting in bed.
Physical Exam: Neurological: Symmetrical reduction in light touch sensation over the bilateral plantar aspects of the feet. Reflexes intact. Cardiovascular: Mild resting tachycardia consistent with anemia, no murmurs. Integumentary: Left deltoid exposed, intact, suitable for injection. The procedure entails administration strictly into the deltoid muscle, followed by post-injection observation for any immediate adverse hypersensitivity reactions. Expected investigation results include complete normalization of serum vitamin B12 levels or total resolution of indices on follow-up haematological testing that would assist in confirming the diagnosis, assuming compliance can be maintained.
Environmental Observations: House is disorganized, curtains drawn; consistent with profound fatigue and agoraphobia.
Medication Reconciliation: Hydroxocobalamin 1mg/1mL ampoule provided by patient (stored correctly).

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Vitamin B12 Intramuscular Injection (Complicated by Symptomatic Relapse and Agoraphobia) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Stroke / TIA - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The peripheral tingling is bilateral, symmetrical, and chronic-progressive, completely lacking the sudden, focal, unilateral deficits characteristic of a vascular event.

Severe Hypothyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a known, established diagnosis of pernicious anemia directly explaining the profound fatigue and neuropathy, specifically worsening after missing the scheduled injection.

Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of chest pain or shortness of breath; tachycardia is mild and compensatory.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Barrier to healthcare access)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a severe, symptomatic relapse of pernicious anemia (profound fatigue, neuropathy) directly resulting from failing to access their critical maintenance injections due to severe agoraphobia. Telephone triage accurately identified that the patient's physical and psychological barriers completely prevented them from utilizing in-hours services. Urgent face-to-face assessment was clinically mandated to perform a neurological assessment, confirm the safety of the patient, and immediately administer the parenteral B12 to arrest the progressing neurological deficits and begin reversing the profound anemia. Deferring this intervention would have allowed the neuropathy to become potentially irreversible and subjected the patient to an unwarranted ED admission for 'collapse query cause'.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Hydroxocobalamin 1mg via deep intramuscular injection into the left deltoid muscle using a 23G needle. Provided post-vaccination observation for 15 minutes; no immediate adverse hypersensitivity reactions occurred. Discussed the critical importance of regular dosing to prevent permanent nerve damage.
Positive Behavioral Change: To bypass your anxiety about leaving the house for your injections, commit right now to setting up an automated "Home Nursing" referral. Keep the empty B12 ampoule box physically taped to your bathroom mirror. This highly visible cue acts as a daily reminder of your absolute need for the medication, reinforcing the necessity to authorize the home nursing service to come to you regularly, completely removing the stressful barrier of travelling to a clinic.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral generated for regular community nursing visits to administer future B12 injections at home.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP telehealth review in 14 days to assess the resolution of the fatigue and tingling, and to confirm the community nursing arrangement.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you experience sudden, severe chest pain, sudden shortness of breath, or if you completely lose the ability to move your legs.

